{
  "decidedBy": "the project owner, 2026-09-30, asking that each king of the united kingdom, of Israel and of Judah carry what the text says of his doing — right, evil or mixed — with the verse it is said in, the age the text gives him, and the carryings away and the return",
  "policy": "A king is marked right, evil or mixed. Where the text says of the king himself that he did right, or did evil, in the sight of the LORD — in its own narration, or in a word of the LORD to him — the mark rests on the text and cites those verses. A right the text qualifies is mixed: 'yet not like David', 'only the high places were not removed', a good beginning with a bad end, or a bad beginning with a good end. Wholly evil is evil, and an evil the text only lessens ('but not as the kings of Israel that were before him') stays evil. David is right, with the exception 1KI 15:5 itself names; Saul and Solomon are mixed. Where the text says neither of a king, the mark is a reading of what it tells of him: it is marked a reading, cites the verses it rests on and gives its reason in a sentence. Samuel, Kings and Chronicles are judged apart. A second history stands beside the first where it passes its own judgment on the king or tells of him what the first does not, and a king on whom the two part is mixed. A king of whom the text tells nothing to judge him by is listed unmarked and given no mark. An age is recorded only where a verse gives it, as the Hebrew has it, and two verses giving two ages are both kept. A carrying away or a return is dated only by the ruler's year its verse gives, and names the event the chronologies already date where they hold it.",
  "method": "manual",
  "source": "Essenthos, on the project owner's request of 2026-09-30 that each king carry the text's judgment of him with its verse, the age the text gives him, and the carryings away and the return dated by the rulers' years",
